WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.639
Have you ever shown a friend a movie only
to hear them say "I don't get it," as the

00:00:03.639 --> 00:00:04.639
credits roll?

00:00:04.639 --> 00:00:08.660
Look, everyone has their own tastes and that’s
great — but sometimes the confusion and

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:13.799
disconnect actually comes from the movie itself
being intentionally confusing, high-concept,

00:00:13.799 --> 00:00:15.340
or just clumsily made.

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:19.980
Let's go over some of these films that make
people say "huh?", and pat ourselves on the

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.150
back for the ones we know inside and out.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:23.160
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.280
"Now that’s a proper introduction"

00:00:26.289 --> 00:00:27.830
Arrival

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:33.280
This 2016 alien invasion film offers a twist
on the popular genre by focusing the plot

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.270
on efforts to communicate with the aliens
across a very tricky language barrier.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.969
It's a slower, more low-key storyline.

00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:44.640
We spend most of the movie with Amy Adams’
world renowned linguist character as she tries

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.400
connecting with the interstellar beings — and
begins to understand their complex language.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:54.030
As the movie goes along, we also get to see
flashes of her character in another life,

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:58.390
caring for a daughter that, we as the audience,
know is doomed to die young.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:02.129
While it’s natural to assume that what we're
seeing are flashbacks, it turns out we're

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:05.040
witnessing a tragedy that actually hasn't
happened yet.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.890
We learn at the film's climax — along with
Adams' character — that the aliens have

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:13.190
their own more circular way of perceiving
time, and the bond that they build with Adams

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:16.140
ends up allowing her to see into the future
as they do.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:21.860
While it's among 2016's most critically acclaimed
wide releases, Arrival earned a B Cinemascore,

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:25.590
suggesting a decent number of people walked
away somewhat confused.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:29.930
With its complicated, somewhat challenging
plot-twist, that's understandable, but ultimately

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:30.930
a shame.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:33.090
This flick is truly a cinematic joy.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:35.780
And then, on the other side of the spectrum...

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.040
Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.040
Oh boy.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.010
This movie isn't confusing because it's too
smart for the audience or anything like that

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:45.540
— it's mostly that it just doesn't make
any sense.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.299
From Wonder Woman's motivations to the basic
structure of the plot — Batman v Superman

00:01:50.299 --> 00:01:55.640
is a movie that barely hangs together, and
only scrapes by thanks to sound and spectacle.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.731
The heavily criticized Martha moment wasn't
just a head-scratching failure because it

00:01:59.731 --> 00:02:01.829
was weird — even though it totally was.

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:05.800
The real problem was that it just wasn't enough
to explain why Batman would suddenly decide

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:08.780
to not fulfill a mission he'd pursued obsessively
to this point.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:16.910
This movie does a lot to convince us that
Batman believes, with absolute certainty,

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:19.040
that Superman is a danger to the world.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.080
So, it really doesn’t make sense that having
a mother with the same name would be enough

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:27.930
to deter Batman — and even have him describe
himself as Superman's "friend" just a few

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:28.930
scenes later.

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:31.480
"I'm a friend of your sons."

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:32.910
"I figured.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:33.910
The cape."

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:38.710
This, coupled with the movie's unexplained
dream sequences and Jesse Eisenberg’s nutso

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:44.870
version of Lex Luthor makes the film feel
fundamentally illogical, incomplete, and frustrating.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:48.780
Lots of longtime fans of these characters
were pretty upset with how the movie handled

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:49.950
the source material.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:55.280
And if Batman v Superman was someone's introduction
to the DC universe, they probably left the

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.210
theater wondering what the hell they just
got themselves into.

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:00.920
Only God Forgives

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:06.230
Nicolas Winding Refn and Ryan Gosling followed
up their dreamy and fantastic film Drive with

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:10.710
a hard left turn into the abstract with 2013’s
Only God Forgives.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:16.150
Taking the action to Thailand, Gosling stars
as, essentially, a way less interesting, non-driving

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:18.130
version of his character from Drive.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:22.760
He spends a big chunk of the movie wandering
through the dreamy Bangkok cityscape, leaving

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.590
audiences to wonder "What the hell is going
on?"

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:30.400
If that wasn't weird and off-putting enough,
we're also treated to surreal karaoke sequences

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.880
from a character called both Lieutenant Chang
and the Angel of Death, and described by the

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:39.810
director as, quote, "a mythological creature
that has a mysterious past but cannot relate

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:42.850
to reality because he's heightened and he's
pure fetish."

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:44.230
Uh, okay.

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:48.680
The entire movie is kinda like that, and it's
not hard to imagine that some folks left the

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:52.340
theater feeling worried that they weren’t
smart enough to understand it.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:56.400
Well, here's some good news: there’s not
actually much going on in this over-blown

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:57.400
film.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.490
For anyone who left the movie feeling confused,
don't sweat it: you were totally justified.

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:04.000
None of this makes any sense.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.480
Prometheus

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:09.521
Fans and critics alike have talked about this
movie’s puzzling failures quite a bit, but

00:04:09.521 --> 00:04:13.200
there are plenty of good reasons to do so
— especially when you consider just how

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.150
much potential Prometheus had.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:20.150
The film is an ambiguous prequel in the Alien
film franchise, but it unfortunately confused

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:26.020
audiences with everything from it characters'
basic motivations to its strange conclusion.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.860
"I think there might be some confusion about
our relationship"

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:34.090
The truth is that Prometheus almost works
— but it falls short as it spends a lot

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:39.220
of time hinting at plot and story, while failing
to actually deliver the necessary parts that

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:40.680
make an audience care.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:45.020
Even longtime fans were unable to connect
to anything happening in front of them during

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:46.020
Prometheus.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:50.520
It was so weird and fundamentally unsatisfying
that it's no surprise the franchise is eager

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.310
to get some distance from this one.

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:56.930
Prometheus 2 has been renamed Alien: Covenant,
and fans' hopes are high that it can bring

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:59.250
the franchise back to form.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.160
The Butterfly Effect

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:05.680
Serious actor Ashton Kutcher's The Butterfly
Effect is one of the dumbest movies ever to

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.100
remain essentially watchable while being completely
illogical at the same time.

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:13.760
While you might think the movie makes sense,
if you take even a second to really think

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.710
about the plot, it totally falls apart.

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:21.960
Kutcher's protagonist is blessed — or perhaps…cursed
— with the ability to use old personal tokens

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.340
to phase back through time to memorable moments
in his life.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:30.270
And so he spends the bulk of the movie trying
to fix his mistakes, usually only to emerge

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:32.450
in situations that are even worse.

00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:36.460
The premise and baseline rule of the movie
— that the slightest change can alter the

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:40.560
course of your life entirely — gets thrown
out the window time and time again whenever

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:42.360
the script deems it necessary.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:47.090
In one scene, as Kutcher tries to defend himself
inside a violent prison, he goes back in time

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:50.820
to stab his hands, frightening the other inmates
with his sudden new wounds.

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:55.639
But if going back in time to change the slightest
thing changes everything, then why is Kutcher

00:05:55.639 --> 00:05:56.639
still in jail?

00:05:56.639 --> 00:06:00.710
Why did the wounds appear as if by magic,
instead of appearing scarred over after what

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:02.950
should have been an entire life of having
them?

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:05.990
Is it really worth it to try and figure out
this movie's logic?

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:07.420
Well… no.

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:11.330
So if you don't get this one, don't worry
too much — no one really does.

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:15.889
It's a great example of a pretty good idea
that ultimately was poorly executed and left

00:06:15.889 --> 00:06:17.240
much to be desired.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:18.820
Ah, well.

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:19.820
Thanks for watching!

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:22.500
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.760
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

